{"reference": "D", "saved_D_answer": "D", "saved_D_rationale": "Answer: D\nReason: The chest X-ray shows bilateral, multifocal peripheral consolidations and nodular opacities with a relatively indolent appearance and preserved lung volumes, which is a classic radiological presentation for low-grade B-cell lymphoma of bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue (BALT lymphoma). While organizing pneumonia can present similarly, the specific combination of persistent, non-segmental peripheral masses/consolidations in this context is most characteristic of pulmonary MALT/BALT lymphoma."}
